  "final_text": "Your sleep over the last 3 weeks (26 Feb to 18 Mar) averaged about 7 hours a night, but some of the Garmin data looks unreliable.\n\n**Duration**\n- The average was about 7.0 hours a night.\n- The best nights were 8.1 h (3 Mar) and 8.0 h (10 Mar).\n- Two nights were under 6 hours: 5.9 h (7 Mar) and 5.7 h (17 Mar, the shortest).\n- Four more nights were between 6.1 and 6.4 hours: 1 Mar, 11 Mar, 16 Mar and last night (6.25 h).\n- Duration swings between 6 and 8 hours from night to night.\n- The last three nights were 6.2, 5.7 and 6.25 hours, so you've built up a short-sleep streak.\n\n**Sleep stages**\n- Stage mix was steady at about 17% deep, 63% light and 20% REM.\n- Last night was slightly better, at 19% deep and 21% REM. You were awake for about 26 minutes, longer than on earlier nights.\n\n**Other metrics**\n- Every night from 26 Feb to 17 Mar shows exactly the same values:\n  - sleep score 63 (\"Fair\")\n  - sleep stress 19\n  - overnight HRV 61\n  - average SpO2 94%, with a low of 87%\n  - 13 minutes awake and 2 awakenings\n- Real nights don't repeat like that, so I wouldn't trust these values. They may be placeholders or a sync problem. The sleep durations do vary, so they may be real.\n- Last night (18 Mar) looks like genuine data: score 74 (Fair), sleep stress 14, HRV 58, SpO2 95%.\n- Because of this, I can't tell you whether your sleep quality or HRV changed over the 3 weeks. Last night's HRV of 58 is only a single reading.\n\n**Takeaway**\n- Your sleep time is adequate on average but inconsistent, and it has dipped over the last few days.\n- If you want to train hard today, a quick check of your readiness would help.\n- It's also worth checking that your watch is syncing properly.",
